# The owner reviews only two kinds of path (workspace#4323); every other path merges on the reviewer
# App's approval and green CI, and a host change that merges still reaches a host only through the
# gated apply. The owner has extended trust to agents on the edge, Caddy, firewall, mail host,
# Ansible roles and playbooks, inventories, Pulumi programs, hetzner/ and ovh/ code, ssh and sudoers
# roles, and secrets handling code, so none of those is listed.
#   (a) Guardrails: a path whose silent weakening would stop an agent guardrail or a required check
#       protecting us: this file and its proof (ansible/checks/test_codeowners.py and
#       codeowners_match.py), CLAUDE.md, .claude/, the workflows, actions and dependabot settings
#       (/.github/, by the standards lines), the gate scripts those checks run (the checks in
#       ansible/checks/, ansible/harness/, the six gates in scripts/ and the named hetzner/scripts
#       checks), the apply-gate allowlists (ansible/checks/host_roles/, ansible/trust/, the signer
#       include), the guards that stop a destructive apply, the floors and lint settings, the image
#       verifier, and the exemption files. The tests of a gate are not listed: they are released.
#   (b) Human-facing documents: README, the licences, and the launch page copy.
# When in doubt: if this file were silently weakened, would a guardrail or a required check stop
# protecting us? Yes, owner. No, released.
# The last matching line wins and there is no catch-all. Blocks are in order, and no block mixes
# owned and released rows. A release is a kind of path from RELEASE_KINDS in
# ansible/checks/test_codeowners.py or one file named in full. The shared standards lines are last
# (SYNC-1). `codeowners_match.py --against OLD` lists every tracked file that lost or gained an owner.

